1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,100
 Hey everyone, this is Dr. Ryan here.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,620
 If you're new to mental dental, welcome to the channel.

3
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:13,320
 I made a video about this very topic, Basic Dental Terminology, about two years ago, and

4
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:18,600
 I've gotten a ton of feedback from it, so now I'm making an updated video.

5
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:23,120
 You might be a dental student, a pre-dental student, a dental hygiene student, a dental

6
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:28,920
 patient, but wherever you are in your dental journey, welcome to this video and I sincerely

7
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:31,860
 hope that you learn a lot about the field.

8
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:39,240
 So with that, let's talk about dental terms used every day in the field of dentistry.

9
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:42,840
 So first, let's get oriented with the mouth.

10
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:49,000
 Of course, we have the lips here that form the outer border of the mouth.

11
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,980
 We have the teeth, we have the tongue, we have the roof of the mouth, which is also

12
00:00:54,980 --> 00:01:00,920
 called the palate, which is divided into a hard palate, which is towards the front of

13
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:06,480
 the mouth, and has underlying bone, and the soft palate, which is towards the back of

14
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:13,020
 the mouth, and includes this hanging bit of soft tissue called the uvula.

15
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:22,020
 The tonsils are also in the back of the mouth, and they serve as part of the immune system.

16
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:27,740
 Now we've separated the teeth from the previous diagram, and the patient is open as wide as

17
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:34,440
 possible, and we have the upper teeth up here and the lower teeth down here.

18
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:40,600
 And note how the teeth form an arch, or an arch, like you'd see in certain architecture.

19
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:46,960
 So we call this one the upper arch, or the maxillary arch, because the upper jaw that

20
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:53,440
 holds these teeth is called the maxilla, and we call this one the lower arch, or the

21
00:01:53,440 --> 00:02:00,480
 mandibular arch, because the lower jaw that holds these teeth is called the mandible.

22
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:03,800
 Now we all have four different kinds of teeth.

23
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:06,600
 The first kind are called the incisors.

24
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:12,080
 We have four incisors in the front of the mouth for the maxillary arch, and also four

25
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:16,560
 incisors down here in the front for the mandibular arch.

26
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:23,200
 There are two central incisors and two lateral incisors in each arch.

27
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:29,120
 These are used for incising or slicing food, like when we bite into an apple, for instance.

28
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:34,400
 Of course, the four upper incisors also play a big role in how our smile looks, and they

29
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:40,000
 help with speech like F and V sounds, for example.

30
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:48,320
 The canines are next, and we have two of them in the upper arch right next door to the incisors,

31
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:51,720
 and we also have two in the lower arch.

32
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:57,360
 They're also called the eye teeth, because they're located in line with your eyes.

33
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:02,960
 They're used for tearing and holding food, and also contribute to the aesthetics of someone's

34
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:06,280
 smile, in the case of the upper canines.

35
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:13,520
 The premolars are next, and we have both a first and a second premolar in each quadrant

36
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:21,760
 of the mouth, so we have two premolars here, two premolars here, and then we have two and

37
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:27,360
 two down here, and again we have a first and a second premolar in every quadrant of the

38
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:28,360
 mouth.

39
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:35,280
 They're used for tearing, holding, and grinding food, and lastly we have the molars, so we

40
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:39,000
 have three molars in each quadrant of the mouth.

41
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,760
 We have a first, a second, and a third molar.

42
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:47,400
 The third molars are also called the wisdom teeth, and they're the most commonly missing

43
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:50,000
 teeth in the mouth.

44
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,440
 Molars are used for grinding and mashing food.

45
00:03:54,440 --> 00:04:02,040
 I should mention that these are the adult teeth, so let's talk about the baby teeth.

46
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:10,720
 The baby teeth also called the primary dentition are shown here, and we have 20 of them in

47
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:11,920
 total.

48
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:15,360
 We have 20 baby teeth when all of them come in.

49
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:24,720
 There are four incisors per arch, two canines per arch, and four molars per arch.

50
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:29,360
 Note that there are no premolars in the baby teeth.

51
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:34,680
 These start coming in at around six months of age, and teeth coming into the mouth is

52
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:37,840
 called eruption.

53
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:42,320
 And all of the baby teeth should have fallen out by at around age 12.

54
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:46,720
 Teeth falling out of the mouth is called exfoliation.

55
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:52,320
 Over on the right we have the adult teeth, also called the permanent dentition.

56
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:55,080
 We have 32 of them in total.

57
00:04:55,080 --> 00:05:00,280
 Of course some people have extra, and some people have missing teeth.

58
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:05,640
 These teeth start to erupt at around age six, and then hopefully they never exfoliate as

59
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:09,960
 long as our teeth remain healthy.

60
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:15,160
 Alright so I told you there are 32 adult teeth, but how do we number them?

61
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:20,600
 Well there are three main numbering systems that dental professionals use today, depending

62
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:23,160
 on where you live in the world.

63
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:29,880
 The FDI system is used in most places in the world, just like the metric system.

64
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:35,120
 The universal numbering system is used in the United States, and the polymer notation is

65
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:40,120
 used in certain specialties, particularly orthodontics and oral surgery.

66
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:42,440
 And I'll tell you why in just a second.

67
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:47,960
 Let's start with the universal system first, and it's just like reading a book.

68
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:54,200
 You start in the top left corner, which is this tooth right here, and then you go from

69
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:56,600
 left to right.

70
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:01,500
 So I want to point out really quick that this side is the patient's right side, and this

71
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:06,320
 is their left side, because remember we're looking at the patient straight on.

72
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:12,520
 So tooth number one is right here, and that's the upper right third molar.

73
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:17,820
 Then we just go in order from there, tooth number two, number three, number four, and

74
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:19,120
 so on.

75
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:24,440
 So we'd go around the upper arch until we got to this tooth over here, which is the upper

76
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:27,960
 left third molar, or tooth number 16.

77
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:33,520
 After that we jump down to the lower arch, and that lower left third molar is tooth number

78
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:40,360
 17, and then we continue numbering here 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, keep going, all the way around

79
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:45,440
 to the lower right third molar, which is tooth number 32.

80
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:50,440
 So the upper right third molar is always tooth number one, the upper right central incisor

81
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:56,960
 is always tooth number eight, and so every tooth has a predetermined number from one

82
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:59,440
 to 32.

83
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:04,920
 For the Palmer notation, we break the mouth into four distinct quadrants.

84
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:09,960
 The vertical line straight down the center of the mouth is called the midline.

85
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:14,840
 This horizontal line is simply separating the maxilla and the mandible.

86
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:20,120
 The Palmer notation is a little bit different in that we don't go above the number eight,

87
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:23,400
 because each quadrant has eight teeth.

88
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:26,960
 We start at the midline and work our way out.

89
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:31,300
 So the central incisors are always tooth number one.

90
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:36,360
 The lateral incisors are always going to be tooth number two, canine will be three, first

91
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:43,000
 premolar will be four, second premolar will be five, then first molar, second molar, and

92
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:47,200
 third molar, and that's true for every quadrant in the mouth.

93
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:52,080
 So we could do this for every single quadrant.

94
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:56,420
 Now you might be wondering, well, how do we distinguish between the ones, the twos, and

95
00:07:56,420 --> 00:08:00,120
 all the other teeth among the different quadrants?

96
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:02,960
 Well notice how the lines intersect.

97
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:12,320
 So this corner forms a backwards L, this corner forms a normal L, this one forms an upside

98
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:17,200
 down L, and then this one over here is an upside down reverse L.

99
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:21,880
 So every quadrant gets a unique symbol.

100
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:27,800
 So now all you have to do is take the symbol for that quadrant and then assign a number

101
00:08:27,800 --> 00:08:31,080
 based on the tooth we're talking about.

102
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:37,720
 So if we're talking about this upper right central incisor, we would just put a one within

103
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:38,720
 this symbol.

104
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:44,040
 If we're talking about this upper left canine, we would put a three among this symbol.

105
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:49,120
 If we're talking about this first premolar and this quadrant, we would just add a four,

106
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:53,560
 and then down here in the lower right, if we're talking about let's say the second molar,

107
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:58,000
 we would just put a seven in that symbol.

108
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:04,360
 So an oral surgeon might get a referral from the orthodontist to extract the upper and

109
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:10,240
 lower fours, and that means the first premolars.

110
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:16,200
 Now that's a whole lot easier than saying please extract tooth number five, number 12,

111
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:18,920
 number 21, and number 28.

112
00:09:18,920 --> 00:09:25,800
 So you can see how the symmetry and the simplicity of this system might be preferred by orthodontist

113
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:28,880
 and oral surgeons for example.

114
00:09:28,880 --> 00:09:36,360
 And lastly, we have the FDI World Dental Federation System, which uses two digits.

115
00:09:36,360 --> 00:09:40,680
 The first digit tells you the quadrant that you're in.

116
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:46,040
 The upper right is quadrant number one, upper left is quadrant number two, lower left is

117
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:51,440
 quadrant number three, and lower right is quadrant number four.

118
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:58,140
 Now the second digit is the exact same as the Palmer notation, one through eight, one

119
00:09:58,140 --> 00:10:01,920
 being central incisor, eight being third molar.

120
00:10:01,920 --> 00:10:06,680
 So now instead of using the symbols here, we just use a number.

121
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:14,080
 So for the upper right central incisor, we would call this tooth number 11, the first

122
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:17,400
 one for the quadrant, the second one for the number.

123
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:25,760
 First pick this lower left second premolar, this would be tooth number 35.

124
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:30,880
 And so you can do this for every quadrant of the mouth using that method.

125
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:36,920
 So if you're curious, this is how we number the primary dentition or the baby teeth.

126
00:10:36,920 --> 00:10:41,960
 For the universal system, instead of using numbers, we use letters.

127
00:10:41,960 --> 00:10:48,020
 So instead of number one to twenty, because there are twenty baby teeth, we use letter

128
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:53,400
 A through J. And the order of notation is otherwise the same.

129
00:10:53,400 --> 00:11:01,720
 So we would start with A in the top right, and then we would continue B, C, D, E, F,

130
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:11,520
 G, H, I, J. Then we would jump down here to tooth K, L, M, N, O, P, Q, R, S, T.

131
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:15,720
 Now for the Palmer system, we also replaced the numbers with letters.

132
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:21,640
 So instead of one to five, because there are now five teeth in each quadrant, it's A

133
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:29,040
 to E. And we use the exact same quadrant symbols that we used before.

134
00:11:29,040 --> 00:11:31,200
 For FDI, it's a little bit different.

135
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:34,120
 We still use two digits.

136
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:41,760
 But for primary teeth, the quadrants are five, six, seven, and eight, instead of one, two,

137
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:43,680
 three, four.

138
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:49,800
 The second digit would just go from one to five, like in the Palmer system.

139
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:53,400
 So those are the three most common tooth numbering systems.

140
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:58,840
 So next time you visit your dentist, you can impress them by pointing out a certain tooth

141
00:11:58,840 --> 00:12:02,560
 and its number.

142
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:05,280
 So now let's talk about the tooth surfaces.

143
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:09,960
 So a tooth consists of both a crown and a root.

144
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:15,720
 The crown is the part above the gums, the root is the part buried below the gums.

145
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:21,440
 The crown of the tooth has different surfaces on it, just like a cube has six surfaces,

146
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:23,320
 so does the tooth's crown.

147
00:12:23,320 --> 00:12:29,840
 There's a front and a back, a left and a right, and a top and a bottom.

148
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:35,520
 And every surface has a name except for the bottom because it's connected directly to

149
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:37,640
 the root underneath.

150
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:42,480
 So the front of the tooth is called the facial surface.

151
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:48,040
 If we look at our diagram over here on the left, this would be the facial surface for

152
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:50,120
 tooth number three.

153
00:12:50,120 --> 00:12:56,000
 This would be the facial surface for tooth number eight.

154
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:00,560
 And this is the surface of the tooth closest to the face, hence why it's called the facial

155
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:01,560
 surface.

156
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:06,360
 Interestingly, there are other names that can be used to describe this surface depending

157
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:08,800
 on where you are in the mouth.

158
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:15,280
 So if you're talking about a front tooth or an anterior tooth that refers to the incisors

159
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:22,640
 as well as the canines, this facial surface can also be called the labial surface.

160
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:25,200
 That's because it's adjacent to the lips.

161
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:30,240
 If you're talking about the back teeth or the posterior teeth that includes the premolars

162
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:36,200
 and the molars, the facial surface can also be called the buckle surface, which is another

163
00:13:36,200 --> 00:13:42,280
 name for the cheeks because those surfaces are adjacent to the cheeks.

164
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:49,000
 The back surface of the tooth is called the lingual surface.

165
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:53,600
 And on the diagram, this would be the lingual surface for this tooth.

166
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:58,960
 And this would be the lingual surface for this tooth.

167
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:04,100
 Now this is the surface of the tooth closest to the tongue, hence why it's called lingual.

168
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:08,120
 In the upper arch, we can also give this a different name.

169
00:14:08,120 --> 00:14:13,440
 We can call it the palatal surface because it's adjacent to the palate, which is the roof

170
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:17,320
 of the mouth up here.

171
00:14:17,320 --> 00:14:26,120
 The side of the tooth that's closest to the midline is called the mezial surface, M for

172
00:14:26,120 --> 00:14:27,320
 midline.

173
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:32,680
 So for tooth number three, this would be the mezial surface, tooth number eight, this would

174
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:34,600
 be the mezial surface.

175
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:43,400
 Of course, all of this is true for the lower arch as well.

176
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:49,320
 Next is the distal surface, that's opposite from mezial, it's the one that's furthest

177
00:14:49,320 --> 00:14:51,240
 away from the midline.

178
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:56,960
 So for tooth number three, this would be the distal surface, tooth number eight, this would

179
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:58,000
 be the distal surface.

180
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:03,720
 And you can imagine that everything would be kind of a mirror image to the side across

181
00:15:03,720 --> 00:15:05,520
 from it.

182
00:15:05,520 --> 00:15:10,480
 So this would be the distal surface for tooth number nine, this would be the distal surface

183
00:15:10,480 --> 00:15:14,280
 for tooth number 14, for instance.

184
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:19,280
 And lastly, we have the chewing surface.

185
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:25,220
 This is called the occlusal surface, which comes from the word occlusion, which refers

186
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:30,360
 to the upper and lower teeth coming together.

187
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:34,520
 Now in the anterior region, this is given a different name.

188
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:38,480
 This is called the incisal surface.

189
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:42,280
 That's true for the incisors as well as the canines.

190
00:15:42,280 --> 00:15:48,440
 In the posterior teeth, these biting surfaces are all the occlusal surfaces.

191
00:15:48,440 --> 00:15:55,880
 All right, so teeth also have different layers.

192
00:15:55,880 --> 00:16:01,960
 We've probably all heard of the enamel layer, and that's the hard calcium-rich surface of

193
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:03,600
 each tooth.

194
00:16:03,600 --> 00:16:07,840
 Enamel is actually the hardest tissue in the human body.

195
00:16:07,840 --> 00:16:15,640
 Denton is the surface underneath the enamel, and it makes up the bulk of these roots here.

196
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:22,320
 Notice how there's no enamel in the root structure.

197
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:27,280
 Denton is a little bit softer and more yellow color than the enamel, because it has more

198
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:30,000
 organic material.

199
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:37,800
 Pulp is the soft tissue inside each tooth, and it contains blood vessels as well as nerves.

200
00:16:37,800 --> 00:16:44,200
 The pulp chamber is the part that's in the crown, and the pulp canal is the part that

201
00:16:44,200 --> 00:16:47,580
 occupies the root.

202
00:16:47,580 --> 00:16:52,240
 The gums are the reddish pink tissue that surrounds each tooth.

203
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:56,520
 It's kind of like the skin around your fingernails and toenails.

204
00:16:56,520 --> 00:17:00,400
 It can be also called the gingerva.

205
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:04,960
 The bone that is underneath the gums and houses and holds each tooth in place by the

206
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:08,720
 roots is called the alveolar bone.

207
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:13,160
 The alveolar bone is part of each jaw bone that we talked about briefly before.

208
00:17:13,160 --> 00:17:15,960
 The upper jaw bone is called the maxilla.

209
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:19,280
 The lower jaw bone is called the mandible.

210
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:27,520
 The cementum refers to the thin, hard tissue that covers the root of each tooth.

211
00:17:27,520 --> 00:17:32,320
 This tissue has similar density to bone.

212
00:17:32,320 --> 00:17:38,360
 Lastly, we have the peridontal ligament, which is this tissue right here.

213
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:42,000
 It's between the cementum and the alveolar bone.

214
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:47,960
 We have lots of ligaments in our body, and they usually connect one bone to another bone.

215
00:17:47,960 --> 00:17:53,920
 In this case, with the peridontal ligament, it connects a tooth, which is not technically

216
00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:58,600
 a bone, to the alveolar bone.

217
00:17:58,600 --> 00:18:04,440
 This decay is the result of germs in our mouth, specifically oral bacteria.

218
00:18:04,440 --> 00:18:10,880
 The bacteria feeds on sugars in our diet, and then they process this sugar to produce acid.

219
00:18:10,880 --> 00:18:16,660
 The acid melts away the mineral content in our enamel, and then our dentin.

220
00:18:16,660 --> 00:18:22,520
 This starts out small and unnoticeable, but then can grow to produce these big holes and

221
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:23,520
 cavities.

222
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:29,480
 The loss of mineral of the tooth due to the byproducts of bacteria is called caries, which

223
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:36,280
 comes from the Latin cariosis, which means full of decay.

224
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:41,480
 Gum disease is the other main disease process that occurs in our mouths.

225
00:18:41,480 --> 00:18:48,800
 Plaque refers to a collection of bacteria on the tooth surface, also called biofilm.

226
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:54,160
 If this plaque is not removed by mechanical brushing and flossing, the bacteria can not

227
00:18:54,160 --> 00:18:59,960
 only melt away our tooth structure like in the caries, but can also anger our gum tissue,

228
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:06,720
 which is called inflammation, which can make them red, swollen, and even painful.

229
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:13,080
 Gingivitis is an early form of gum disease, where the gums become inflamed, so they're

230
00:19:13,080 --> 00:19:16,080
 red, swollen, and can bleed easily.

231
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:21,400
 Again, it's usually caused by plaque buildup near the gum tissue.

232
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:26,880
 If this plaque stays around for a long period of time, the situation can get even worse,

233
00:19:26,880 --> 00:19:34,200
 and then we get to what's called periodontitis, which is a more severe infection of the gums.

234
00:19:34,200 --> 00:19:40,000
 It involves not only gingival inflammation, but the gums and the bone that supports the

235
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:46,280
 teeth can retreat and recede away from the plaque.

236
00:19:46,280 --> 00:19:55,160
 When severe or chronic, teeth can loosen over time and even fall out if enough bone is lost.

237
00:19:55,160 --> 00:20:02,640
 So the difference is gingivitis is reversible, while periodontitis is irreversible.

238
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:05,880
 The names of each also explain exactly what they are.

239
00:20:05,880 --> 00:20:13,680
 This means inflammation, so gingivitis means inflammation of the gums, and periodontitis

240
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:21,120
 means inflammation of the tissues that surround the tooth.

241
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:25,800
 Now let's go over some specific terminology for dental treatment.

242
00:20:25,800 --> 00:20:31,640
 So restoration refers to any kind of treatment that repairs or places teeth.

243
00:20:31,640 --> 00:20:36,540
 So this can include fillings that replace decayed or missing tooth structure, but this

244
00:20:36,540 --> 00:20:41,000
 also includes some bigger procedures like crowns and bridges that we'll talk about in

245
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,320
 a little bit.

246
00:20:43,320 --> 00:20:49,080
 So amalgam is a dental filling material made up of a mixture of different metals, such

247
00:20:49,080 --> 00:20:54,800
 as mercury, silver, tin, and copper, also known as a silver filling.

248
00:20:54,800 --> 00:21:01,440
 Composite is a tooth-colored filling material used to repair or cosmetically enhance teeth

249
00:21:01,440 --> 00:21:06,560
 that's made of several types of resin-based plastic substances.

250
00:21:06,560 --> 00:21:11,400
 Bonding is another term that some dentists may use, and it refers to the process by which

251
00:21:11,400 --> 00:21:16,520
 a tooth-colored filling material is attached to the tooth.

252
00:21:16,520 --> 00:21:22,160
 A sealant is a thin plastic resin coating that can be placed on the biting surfaces of

253
00:21:22,160 --> 00:21:30,600
 back teeth to help prevent carries from forming within the pits and fissures of these teeth.

254
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:36,080
 A crown is the top part of a natural tooth that's covered with enamel, but it's also

255
00:21:36,080 --> 00:21:42,280
 the name for a filling that covers the entire natural crown when the tooth has broken down

256
00:21:42,280 --> 00:21:47,640
 and can't be fixed by a smaller amalgam or composite filling.

257
00:21:47,640 --> 00:21:55,760
 Brids can be ceramic to match tooth color or they can be gold metal like this crown here.

258
00:21:55,760 --> 00:22:01,960
 A bridge is an appliance that is cemented in place, just like a crown, but it replaces

259
00:22:01,960 --> 00:22:03,480
 missing teeth.

260
00:22:03,480 --> 00:22:09,120
 So if this tooth was completely missing, for example, the bridge would cover the adjacent

261
00:22:09,120 --> 00:22:17,520
 teeth and then a fake tooth would be placed in this empty space to replace the missing

262
00:22:17,520 --> 00:22:21,880
 tooth and bridge the gap there.

263
00:22:21,880 --> 00:22:27,160
 Another way to replace a missing tooth or teeth is with a denture.

264
00:22:27,160 --> 00:22:32,040
 So this is something that's taken in and out by the patient and can be a partial denture

265
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:38,840
 like this one to replace some missing teeth or it can be a complete denture if all of

266
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:41,680
 the teeth of that arch are missing.

267
00:22:41,680 --> 00:22:47,180
 An implant is another option for replacing a missing tooth and it's a device that's

268
00:22:47,180 --> 00:22:53,520
 inserted into the jawbone to replace a missing tooth and hold a crown or a bridge or even

269
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:56,840
 anchor a denture in place.

270
00:22:56,840 --> 00:23:04,120
 An extraction is the removal of all or part of a tooth.

271
00:23:04,120 --> 00:23:07,480
 So here we see a root canal treatment.

272
00:23:07,480 --> 00:23:13,760
 So everyone has root canals, but only those with deep enough decay or trauma require a

273
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:16,160
 root canal treatment.

274
00:23:16,160 --> 00:23:21,880
 This refers to a treatment that removes the tooth nerve or the pulp tissue and it seals

275
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:27,120
 the space with a biologically stable material.

276
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:32,840
 A crown is usually recommended to cover a back tooth when it gets a root canal treatment

277
00:23:32,840 --> 00:23:36,000
 to prevent it from breaking down.

278
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:42,800
 A veneer is a thin artificial cover for a tooth to correct its shape or color.

279
00:23:42,800 --> 00:23:47,600
 Veneers can be made of ceramic, composite, or acrylic resin.

280
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:54,280
 Scaling is a procedure that uses instruments to remove plaque, tartar and stains from teeth,

281
00:23:54,280 --> 00:24:00,720
 and root planing refers to the cleaning of a tooth root to remove bacteria, calculus,

282
00:24:00,720 --> 00:24:07,400
 and diseased cementum surfaces.

283
00:24:07,400 --> 00:24:12,880
 So a general dentist can provide the treatments we just talked about, but there are specialists

284
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:18,880
 who receive specialized training for dealing with more complex cases.

285
00:24:18,880 --> 00:24:24,680
 There are more specialties being added every year, but as of recording this video in 2020,

286
00:24:24,680 --> 00:24:29,880
 there are 12 ADA-recognized dental specialties.

287
00:24:29,880 --> 00:24:36,000
 Dental anesthesiologists are specially trained to provide pain management during dental procedures,

288
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:43,360
 particularly things like intravenous, deep sedation, and general anesthesia.

289
00:24:43,360 --> 00:24:48,400
 Dental public health specialists focus on advocating for dental and oral health issues

290
00:24:48,400 --> 00:24:52,760
 at the community level, things like water fluoridation.

291
00:24:52,760 --> 00:24:59,480
 Endodontists treat problems of the tooth, nerve, or pulp, or infections in the bone associated

292
00:24:59,480 --> 00:25:06,640
 with infected nerves, with procedures such as root canal treatments.

293
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:12,480
 Oral medicine doctors diagnose and manage patients with disorders of the face and mouth, and

294
00:25:12,480 --> 00:25:18,680
 provide biopsies, medical management, and specialized injections.

295
00:25:18,680 --> 00:25:26,800
 Oral pathologists specialize in the research, identification, and management of oral disease.

296
00:25:26,800 --> 00:25:33,920
 Oral radiologists specialize in the production and interpretation of dental x-rays.

297
00:25:33,920 --> 00:25:40,480
 Oral surgeons perform tooth extractions, trauma surgery, corrective jaw surgery, and implant

298
00:25:40,480 --> 00:25:42,680
 placements.

299
00:25:42,680 --> 00:25:47,760
 Orophacial pain specialists are specially trained to diagnose and manage head and neck

300
00:25:47,760 --> 00:25:53,160
 pain, like headaches, migraines, and jaw pain.

301
00:25:53,160 --> 00:25:59,280
 Endodontists correct the position of teeth with braces and other appliances.

302
00:25:59,280 --> 00:26:04,080
 Pediatric dentists specialize in treating children and patients with special healthcare

303
00:26:04,080 --> 00:26:06,080
 needs.

304
00:26:06,080 --> 00:26:10,920
 Periodontists specialize in treating the periodontal tissues that surround the teeth, so this

305
00:26:10,920 --> 00:26:16,160
 includes the gums, the periodontal ligament, bone, and even the cementum.

306
00:26:16,160 --> 00:26:22,760
 And prosthodontists replace missing teeth with crowns, bridges, dentures, and the restoration

307
00:26:22,760 --> 00:26:24,320
 of implants.

308
00:26:24,320 --> 00:26:33,720
 These artificial teeth replacements are known as prostheses, hence the name prosthodontists.

309
00:26:33,720 --> 00:26:38,320
 And lastly, there are a few more miscellaneous terms that I'd like to review listed here

310
00:26:38,320 --> 00:26:40,120
 in alphabetical order.

311
00:26:40,120 --> 00:26:42,280
 First, we have abrasion.

312
00:26:42,280 --> 00:26:47,400
 This refers to wear on a tooth, usually caused by brushing too hard or using a toothpaste

313
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:49,840
 that's too abrasive.

314
00:26:49,840 --> 00:26:56,420
 This is what it can look like if you scrub back and forth too hard with your toothbrush.

315
00:26:56,420 --> 00:27:02,120
 A band refers to a metal ring placed around a tooth with cement as part of orthodontic

316
00:27:02,120 --> 00:27:03,120
 treatment.

317
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:10,200
 A bite wing is an x-ray of the crowns of the upper and lower molars and premolars.

318
00:27:10,200 --> 00:27:15,240
 It's the most common x-ray taken at a routine exam.

319
00:27:15,240 --> 00:27:20,920
 Cruxism refers to an unconscious habit of grinding or clenching the teeth, which often

320
00:27:20,920 --> 00:27:25,880
 happens when a person is sleeping or stressed out during the day.

321
00:27:25,880 --> 00:27:33,120
 Calculus or tartar refers to a hard deposit of minerals coated with plaque that can build

322
00:27:33,120 --> 00:27:37,120
 up on the teeth and cause gums to get inflamed.

323
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:43,400
 The most common place for it to accumulate is the lingual surfaces of the lower incisors.

324
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:49,080
 And this is the stuff that's scraped off when a dentist cleans your teeth.

325
00:27:49,080 --> 00:27:55,160
 A cusp is one of the pointed parts on the top of a tooth.

326
00:27:55,160 --> 00:28:04,160
 A premolar can also be called a bicuspid because it has two cusp tips.

327
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:10,340
 Dry socket refers to this phenomenon of pain and inflammation in a tooth socket after the

328
00:28:10,340 --> 00:28:11,640
 tooth is removed.

329
00:28:11,640 --> 00:28:15,520
 And that happens if the blood clot is lost.

330
00:28:15,520 --> 00:28:21,680
 So if the blood clot is dislodged and displaced from the socket, it leaves the bone and the

331
00:28:21,680 --> 00:28:26,920
 nerve endings exposed, which is what causes the pain.

332
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:32,800
 Erosion refers to the loss or wear of enamel by acids not caused by bacteria.

333
00:28:32,800 --> 00:28:40,040
 So this includes things like soda, fruit juice, and stomach acid in the case of acid reflux.

334
00:28:40,040 --> 00:28:45,440
 An impacted tooth refers to a tooth that's blocked from erupting through the gums on

335
00:28:45,440 --> 00:28:46,440
 time.

336
00:28:46,440 --> 00:28:51,420
 And this could be caused by another tooth, bone, or soft tissue.

337
00:28:51,420 --> 00:28:56,800
 Amalocclusion refers to when the upper and lower teeth aren't lined up well in order

338
00:28:56,800 --> 00:28:59,040
 to bite and chew properly.

339
00:28:59,040 --> 00:29:04,160
 Again, just the word occlusion, like we talked about before, refers to the contact between

340
00:29:04,160 --> 00:29:06,440
 the upper and lower teeth.

341
00:29:06,440 --> 00:29:12,440
 An occlusal guard is a removable device that a person wears over their teeth.

342
00:29:12,440 --> 00:29:18,200
 A mouth guard is used to protect them from damage during sports and other activities.

343
00:29:18,200 --> 00:29:24,400
 And a night guard is used to protect them from bruxism during sleep.

344
00:29:24,400 --> 00:29:29,040
 A periodontal pocket is a deep area between a tooth and gum.

345
00:29:29,040 --> 00:29:31,360
 That's the result of gum disease.

346
00:29:31,360 --> 00:29:36,640
 So this is what dentists often measure in millimeters during a routine exam when they

347
00:29:36,640 --> 00:29:42,120
 call out numbers like 1, 3, 4, for instance.

348
00:29:42,120 --> 00:29:47,280
 A retainer is a removable device that's worn in the mouth to prevent teeth from moving

349
00:29:47,280 --> 00:29:48,280
 at a position.

350
00:29:48,280 --> 00:29:54,040
 It's often used after orthodontic treatment or premature loss of teeth.

351
00:29:54,040 --> 00:29:57,480
 Sublingual refers to any area under the tongue.

352
00:29:57,480 --> 00:30:04,160
 There are lots of salivary glands that produce saliva in this area of the mouth.

353
00:30:04,160 --> 00:30:08,760
 TMJ stands for Temparo Mandibular Joint.

354
00:30:08,760 --> 00:30:13,960
 This is a joint that connects the lower jaw to the skull.

355
00:30:13,960 --> 00:30:19,920
 Now TMD stands for Temparo Mandibular Joint Dysfunction.

356
00:30:19,920 --> 00:30:25,800
 So everyone has a TMJ, two in fact, one on the right and one on the left, but not everyone

357
00:30:25,800 --> 00:30:27,960
 has TMD.

358
00:30:27,960 --> 00:30:33,480
 This refers to pain, clicking, popping, and other symptoms that are caused by problems

359
00:30:33,480 --> 00:30:38,600
 with the Temparo Mandibular Joint and the associated muscles.

360
00:30:38,600 --> 00:30:42,800
 And lastly, zerostomia is a fancy word for dry mouth.

361
00:30:42,800 --> 00:30:48,360
 This can be caused by salivary glands that don't work properly or reduced flow of saliva

362
00:30:48,360 --> 00:30:52,680
 from certain medications.

363
00:30:52,680 --> 00:30:55,800
 And that's it for this video guys, thank you so much for watching.

364
00:30:55,800 --> 00:31:00,320
 I really hope you learned a lot about dentistry and that you enjoyed the video.

365
00:31:00,320 --> 00:31:05,880
 If you did, please like this video and subscribe to this channel for much more on dentistry.

366
00:31:05,880 --> 00:31:09,760
 If you're interested in supporting me and what I do here, please check out my Patreon

367
00:31:09,760 --> 00:31:10,760
 page.

368
00:31:10,760 --> 00:31:12,760
 I'll leave a link in the description below.

369
00:31:12,760 --> 00:31:16,200
 Thanks again for watching everyone, and I'll see you all in the next video.

